/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 150 0 R
/T (      Hiraya turned away from the sight. She could not understand how a kingdom could appear so wealthy while leaving so many of its people hungry.)
/E (      Hiraya turned away from the sight. She could not understand how a kingdom could appear so wealthy while leaving so many of its people hungry.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (      As the heir of Raja Lakandula and his first wife, Hara Marikit, Hiraya knows that someday everything in Marilag would be hers to rule and protect. She was taught the traditions of her people, the rules and customs of the court and the responsibilities expected of a future Hara. Until that time, she had believed that as a Dayang it meant learning how to rule. She had yet to understand that ruling and serving were not two different duties.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (     A few days later, Raja Lakandula called a meeting with his trusted datus. The kingdom's problems had grown worse. Several merchant boats had disappeared without a trace, and merchants who once filled the ports of Marilag had begun choosing other routes. Neighboring rulers had also become less willing to trade with them.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (     The council feared that Marilag was slowly being isolated. There was one power, however, that seemed willing to help.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 151 0 R
/T (    The Sultanate of Akbar, a powerful maritime sultanate beyond Marilag's southern waters, had offered grain, ships, and protection for Marilag's trade routes. Its ruler, Sultan Jalal, controlled a powerful fleet, while his eldest son, Ladja Makusug, was the heir to the sultanate. Akbar's offer appeared generous. There was only one condition. Hiraya would marry Makusug.)
/E (    The Sultanate of Akbar, a powerful maritime sultanate beyond Marilag's southern waters, had offered grain, ships, and protection for Marilag's trade routes. Its ruler, Sultan Jalal, controlled a powerful fleet, while his eldest son, Ladja Makusug, was the heir to the sultanate. Akbar's offer appeared generous. There was only one condition. Hiraya would marry Makusug.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 151 0 R
/E (    The marriage, according to the Akbari envoys, would unite the two realms and guarantee peace for generations. Akbar would provide food while Marilag recovered from its poor harvest, and its ships would protect Marilag's waters from pirates and rival kingdoms. As soon as Raja made the offer, Hiraya was almost stunned at the proposal and could hardly believe her own ears. She was not able to fathom the fact that Akbar was willing to offer so much to the kingdom which has so little to offer to him in return.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
